BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:warhol-foundation-exhibition-support-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260905T151909Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260901
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260902
SUMMARY:Deadline: Andy Warhol Foundation: Exhibition Support (Fall 2026 rou
 nd)
DESCRIPTION:Supports solo\, two-person and thematic group exhibitions of co
 ntemporary visual art. The Foundation explicitly prioritises artists whose
  work is under-recognised and whose practices are experimental or hard to 
 categorise\, which makes it a genuine route for media arts\, digital cultu
 re and art-and-science exhibitions rather than a blue-chip-only fund. Gran
 ts of USD 60\,000 to 100\,000 cover direct exhibition costs including arti
 st fees\, new commissions\, catalogue production and artist-designed publi
 c programming\, but not staff salaries. Applicants may request no more tha
 n 25 percent of total direct costs. The proposal is a roughly three-page c
 uratorial letter plus detailed direct-cost budget\, organisational budget 
 and IRS documentation. Deadlines are 1 March and 1 September annually\, wi
 th notifications 1 July and 1 January. IMPORTANT: this is a postmark or em
 ail-timestamp deadline\, not a portal deadline. Fiscal sponsors are explic
 itly not accepted\; the applicant must hold its own IRS ruling letter. The
  six-month-to-two-year timing rule is strict\, so a September 2026 applica
 tion suits exhibitions programmed from mid-2027 onward.\n\nOrganisation: T
 he Andy Warhol Foundation for the Visual Arts\nLocation: Applicant's own v
 enue. US-based 501(c)(3) required\; international organisations must first
  submit a letter of inquiry before any proposal is accepted.\nAward: USD 6
 0\,000 to 100\,000. Direct costs only (artist payments\, catalogue and pub
 lication\, artist-designed public programmes)\; staff salaries excluded. R
 equest must not exceed 25 percent of total direct project costs\, which fu
 nctions as a de facto 75 percent co-financing requirement. No application 
 fee.\nDuration: Exhibition must open between 6 months and 2 years after no
 tification.\n\nMore: https://warholfoundation.org/grants/application-guide
 lines/exhibition-support/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium
 =referral
URL:https://warholfoundation.org/grants/application-guidelines/exhibition-s
 upport/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Applicant's own venue. US-based 501(c)(3) required\; international
  organisations must first submit a letter of inquiry before any proposal i
 s accepted.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:warhol-foundation-multiyear-program-support-2026@artificialnouveau.gith
 ub.io
DTSTAMP:20260905T151909Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260901
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260902
SUMMARY:Deadline: Andy Warhol Foundation: Multi-year Program Support (Fall 
 2026 round)
DESCRIPTION:This is the closest thing in US foundation philanthropy to core
  support for an organisation running an ongoing public programme\, as oppo
 sed to a single exhibition. It funds a two-year slate of exhibitions\, res
 idencies\, public art\, screenings\, performances\, lectures and publicati
 ons\, which means a recurring festival or convening series can be underwri
 tten here. Grants are USD 60\,000 to 100\,000 over two years and may not e
 xceed 25 percent of annual operating budget. The Foundation states a prefe
 rence for organisations supporting artists with experimental\, hard-to-cat
 egorise practices\, putting digital culture\, sound art and art-and-scienc
 e programmers squarely in scope. Applications require a forward programme 
 schedule covering at least the twelve months after the award\, a three-pag
 e narrative focused specifically on how artists benefit (the stated centra
 l evaluation criterion)\, a full one-year organisational budget and 501(c)
 (3) documentation. Deadlines are 1 March and 1 September\, notifications 1
  July and 1 January. Fiscal sponsors are not accepted. Organisations that 
 received a Multi-year grant within the past year are ineligible\, and prop
 osals centred on anniversaries\, relocations or major renovations are expl
 icitly discouraged.\n\nOrganisation: The Andy Warhol Foundation for the Vi
 sual Arts\nLocation: Programming delivered by the applicant organisation. 
 US 501(c)(3) required\; international organisations must submit a letter o
 f inquiry first.\nAward: USD 60\,000 to 100\,000 across two years. Request
  must not exceed 25 percent of the organisation's annual operating budget.
  No formal match\, but the 25 percent cap means the balance must come from
  elsewhere. No application fee.\nDuration: Two years of programming\, begi
 nning after the 1 January 2027 notification.\n\nMore: https://warholfounda
 tion.org/grants/application-guidelines/multi-year-program-support/?utm_sou
 rce=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://warholfoundation.org/grants/application-guidelines/multi-year-p
 rogram-support/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Programming delivered by the applicant organisation. US 501(c)(3) 
 required\; international organisations must submit a letter of inquiry fir
 st.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:warhol-foundation-small-scale-project-grants-2026@artificialnouveau.git
 hub.io
DTSTAMP:20260905T151909Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260901
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260902
SUMMARY:Deadline: Andy Warhol Foundation: Project Grants for Small-Scale Or
 ganizations (Fall 2026 round)
DESCRIPTION:Launched in February 2026 as an expansion of the Foundation's p
 rogramme to reach organisations too small for the Exhibition Support and M
 ulti-year strands\, and it is the single most relevant new US scheme for s
 mall media-arts festivals. It funds visual arts projects with a public com
 ponent\, explicitly naming film festivals\, exhibitions\, installations\, 
 residencies and interdisciplinary collaborations. Grants are USD 20\,000 t
 o 30\,000 toward direct costs. The hard gate is the USD 200\,000 annual op
 erating budget ceiling plus US 501(c)(3) status held directly by the appli
 cant\, not via a fiscal sponsor. The Foundation states it assesses creativ
 e risk-taking and the growth of participating artists rather than communit
 y impact metrics\, which suits experimental sound and digital culture prog
 ramming. Proposals are a maximum of three pages plus a one-year operating 
 budget\, a project budget and IRS documentation. Deadlines are 1 March (pr
 ojects begin 1 July or later) and 1 September (projects begin 1 January or
  later). Youth-focused projects are ineligible. One project per round\, an
 d grantees must wait a year after project completion and final reporting b
 efore reapplying.\n\nOrganisation: The Andy Warhol Foundation for the Visu
 al Arts\nLocation: Applicant's own venue or programme\, with a mandatory p
 ublic component. US-based only.\nAward: USD 20\,000 to 30\,000 toward dire
 ct costs such as artist payments and production. No application fee.\nDura
 tion: Project must complete within one year of notification\; September-ro
 und projects may begin 1 January 2027 or later.\n\nMore: https://warholfou
 ndation.org/grants/application-guidelines/project-grants-for-small-scale-o
 rganizations/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://warholfoundation.org/grants/application-guidelines/project-gran
 ts-for-small-scale-organizations/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&ut
 m_medium=referral
LOCATION:Applicant's own venue or programme\, with a mandatory public compo
 nent. US-based only.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:alabama-clotilda-memorial-public-art-commission-2026@artificialnouveau.
 github.io
DTSTAMP:20260905T151909Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260809
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260810
SUMMARY:Deadline: Alabama Historical Commission: Clotilda Memorial Public A
 rt Commission
DESCRIPTION:The Alabama Historical Commission\, working with public-art con
 sultancy Forecast Public Art\, seeks proposals for a major memorial artwor
 k honoring the survivors of the Clotilda\, the last known ship to illegall
 y traffic enslaved Africans to the United States (1860). The memorial will
  convey the survivors' lives and legacies and be developed through collabo
 rative community engagement with historic Africatown\, the community found
 ed by Clotilda survivors\, and the broader diaspora. ELIGIBILITY: open to 
 artists of all disciplines\, including time-based and media artists\; indi
 viduals and teams\; open internationally. BUDGET: USD 2\,000\,000 total pr
 oject budget. DEADLINE: 9 August 2026\, 11:59pm CST. Applications are hand
 led through the CODAworx RFQ platform\; find the Clotilda Memorial listing
  in the CODAworx opportunities index. Verify the listing details on CODAwo
 rx before applying.\n\nOrganisation: Alabama Historical Commission (with F
 orecast Public Art)\nLocation: Africatown\, Mobile\, Alabama\, USA\; open 
 to artists internationally.\nAward: USD 2\,000\,000 total project budget f
 or a paid public art commission. No application fee.\nDuration: Permanent 
 public art commission\n\nMore: https://www.codaworx.com/rfp/?utm_source=ar
 tificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.codaworx.com/rfp/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm
 _medium=referral
LOCATION:Africatown\, Mobile\, Alabama\, USA\; open to artists internationa
 lly.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:creative-philadelphia-msb-interpretive-panels-2026@artificialnouveau.gi
 thub.io
DTSTAMP:20260905T151909Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260712
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260713
SUMMARY:Deadline: MSB Interpretive Panels: Public Art Commission (Creative 
 Philadelphia)
DESCRIPTION:Creative Philadelphia announces a call to commission a series o
 f permanent interpretive panels with integrated augmented reality (AR) com
 ponents at the Municipal Services Building in Center City Philadelphia. Th
 e commission is part of a broader initiative that includes an upcoming pub
 lic sculpture of Dr. Sadie T.M. Alexander by artist Vinnie Bagwell. Inspir
 ed by Alexander's groundbreaking legacy in public service\, these interpre
 tive panels will celebrate the City of Philadelphia's municipal employees 
 (past\, present\, and future) and provide deeper context for understanding
  Dr. Alexander's impact\; they will be located in front of the building's 
 landscaped areas\, flanking the upcoming sculpture\, and are intended to c
 omplement and support the overall site experience. SCOPE: an artist team w
 ill design and fabricate four (4) permanent\, sculptural interpretive pane
 ls incorporating augmented reality (AR) elements to expand storytelling be
 yond the physical surface of the artwork. ELIGIBILITY: all interested arti
 sts are invited to apply\; Philadelphia-based artists are strongly encoura
 ged\, though artists from outside the region will be considered\; multiple
  in-person site visits are required and associated travel costs must be co
 vered within the project budget. SELECTION: a two-part process: Part 1 sub
 mission of qualifications\; Part 2 finalist proposals and interviews. Up t
 o three (3) finalists are invited to submit short concept proposals and at
 tend an interview\; at no point should artists submit design proposals. BU
 DGET: USD 250\,000 total (see amount for the required cost breakdown). An 
 Artist Informational Meeting is held via Zoom on 30 June\, 6:00-7:00pm. Ap
 plication questions: paige.phillips@phila.gov. Apply via Submittable. Dead
 line 12 July 2026 (submissions close Monday 13 July 2026\, 6:00 AM).\n\nOr
 ganisation: Creative Philadelphia (Office of Arts\, Culture and the Creati
 ve Economy)\nLocation: Municipal Services Building\, Center City\, Philade
 lphia\, Pennsylvania\, United States. Philadelphia-based artists strongly 
 encouraged\; artists from outside the region considered. Multiple in-perso
 n site visits required (travel costs covered within the project budget).\n
 Award: Total project budget of USD 250\,000\, which must cover: artist fee
 s (18% design fee)\, design and engineering\, fabrication and installation
 \, structural footings/anchoring\, travel and site visits\, AR development
  (software\, hosting\, content production)\, insurance (1%)\, community en
 gagement participation\, and a service warranty keeping content compatible
  with new mobile OS versions for a minimum of 10 years. No application fee
 s.\nDuration: Permanent public artwork commission\; two-part selection pro
 cess.\n\nMore: https://oacce.submittable.com/submit/357418/msb-interpretiv
 e-panels?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://oacce.submittable.com/submit/357418/msb-interpretive-panels?utm
 _source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Municipal Services Building\, Center City\, Philadelphia\, Pennsyl
 vania\, United States. Philadelphia-based artists strongly encouraged\; ar
 tists from outside the region considered. Multiple in-person site visits r
 equired (travel costs covered within the project budget).
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:mak-center-artists-architects-residence-2027-2028@artificialnouveau.git
 hub.io
DTSTAMP:20260905T151909Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260812
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260813
SUMMARY:Deadline: MAK Center: Artists and Architects in Residence 2027/2028
  (MAK Schindler Scholarship\, Los Angeles)
DESCRIPTION:The MAK Center for Art and Architecture awards Artists and Arch
 itects in Residence scholarships (the MAK Schindler Scholarship) to indepe
 ndent projects exploring the relation between art and architecture. Projec
 ts are chosen for their ability to critically examine current trends in ar
 t\, architecture and society\; to make a compelling connection with Los An
 geles as the place of implementing or continuing the applicant's own work\
 ; and to demonstrate a conceptual and experimental approach. Emphasis is p
 laced on the L.A. location\, and residents' projects often reflect\, incor
 porate and address specific facets of the city and its social fabric. Reci
 pients are encouraged to take part in the MAK Center's activities and to a
 ttend meetings\, workshops and project discussions with other artists and 
 students. ELIGIBILITY: freelance artists\; fine arts or architecture stude
 nts at a university who have completed the '2. Studienabschnitt'\; or arch
 itects or artists who have recently graduated. Teams of up to three are el
 igible if all members practice together as an artist group or collective o
 f architects\, are all present in L.A. for the six months\, and name a con
 tact responsible for the team. HOUSING: the Mackey Apartments are made ava
 ilable to scholarship holders only\; family members\, relatives and friend
 s may be accommodated for a maximum of two weeks during the term\, permitt
 ed in advance by Beth Stryker\, Director of the MAK Center. Eligibility is
  excluded if the applicant has been or will be granted another scholarship
  in the USA immediately before or after the MAK scholarship\, or if the da
 ta form is incomplete or the required portfolio is missing. Residency term
 s run October to March and April to September. Deadline 12 August 2026. Ap
 ply: https://www.makcenter.org/apply-mackey\n\nOrganisation: MAK Center fo
 r Art and Architecture\, Los Angeles\nLocation: Los Angeles\, California\,
  United States (Mackey Apartments). Residents must be present in L.A. for 
 the full six-month term.\nAward: Six-month MAK Schindler Scholarship resid
 ency with accommodation provided at the Mackey Apartments. The open call d
 oes not state a stipend\, travel or honorarium figure\; confirm any additi
 onal financial support directly with the MAK Center. No application fee st
 ated.\nDuration: Six months. Residency terms run October to March or April
  to September\; 2027/2028 cycle.\n\nMore: https://www.makcenter.org/apply-
 mackey?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.makcenter.org/apply-mackey?utm_source=artificialnouveaugran
 tdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Los Angeles\, California\, United States (Mackey Apartments). Resi
 dents must be present in L.A. for the full six-month term.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:cali-futures-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260905T151909Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260828
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260829
SUMMARY:Deadline: CALI Futures 2026
DESCRIPTION:CALI Futures supports artists and cultural workers across Calif
 ornia\, individually and in teams\, who are meaningfully contributing to a
 lternative efforts outside of conventional nonprofit and for-profit arts a
 nd culture systems. The Fund encourages work focused on income\, ownership
 \, and care\, and uplifts the role of artists and cultural workers in shap
 ing and sustaining alternative efforts that provide greater financial stab
 ility\, strengthen creative ownership\, and deepen mutual support across t
 he broader arts ecosystem. Project-restricted grants of USD 5\,000 are awa
 rded through a competitive process. Competitive applicants present (a) a c
 lear project request\; (b) proof of an active\, current artistic or cultur
 al practice\; (c) a description of their contribution to an alternative ef
 fort that improves financial sustainability\, creative ownership\, or mutu
 al support and addresses challenges not sufficiently solved by conventiona
 l nonprofit or for-profit sectors\; and (d) framing that ambitiously descr
 ibes the larger implications of this work for transforming artists' lives 
 and future cultural possibilities. The Fund supports individuals and teams
  at every stage\, from initial ideas to research\, implementation\, experi
 mentation\, and reflection. ELIGIBILITY: applicants must be individual art
 ists or cultural workers (cultural producers\, culture bearers\, creatives
 \, cultural practitioners)\; must reside full-time in California\; must be
  contributing to an alternative effort improving financial sustainability\
 , creative ownership\, or mutualistic social support within the arts and c
 ulture sector\; and that contribution must have occurred or begun on or af
 ter 1 January 2020. Teams are eligible\, but all team members must meet th
 e criteria and only one application is accepted per team (one member appli
 es and is responsible for grant requirements). INELIGIBLE: individuals wit
 hout an active practice\; organizations seeking operating or program suppo
 rt\; arts administrators\; individuals living or working outside Californi
 a\; past CALI Catalyst grantees describing the same project funded in 2021
 -2025\; incomplete applications\; and those with a conflict of interest wi
 th CCI or Hewlett Foundation board\, staff\, or directors. The Fund is adm
 inistered by the Center for Cultural Innovation (CCI) with funding from th
 e William and Flora Hewlett Foundation. TIMELINE: applications open 9 June
  2026 at 9:00 a.m. PT\; deadline 28 August 2026 at 11:59 a.m. PT\; review 
 September to November 2026\; notifications by 17 November 2026. Submitted 
 via Submittable. Questions: grants@cciarts.org (include CALI FUTURES in th
 e subject line) or 415.288.0530.\n\nOrganisation: Center for Cultural Inno
 vation (CCI)\nLocation: California\, USA. Applicants must reside full-time
  in California\nAward: USD 5\,000 project-restricted grants\, awarded comp
 etitively. USD 100\,000 total available each year across the 2026-2028 fun
 ding cycle. No application fee.\nDuration: Grant support period December 2
 026 to December 2027\n\nMore: https://www.cciarts.org/cgi/page.cgi/cali_fu
 tures.htm?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.cciarts.org/cgi/page.cgi/cali_futures.htm?utm_source=artifi
 cialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:California\, USA. Applicants must reside full-time in California
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:cfa-imgn-grant-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260905T151909Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261231
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20270101
SUMMARY:Deadline: CFA Institute x IMGN Grant 2026
DESCRIPTION:One-off film grant awarding a single filmmaker $4\,500 to suppo
 rt an independent narrative project in 2026. Application is free to submit
 . Centerpiece of the application is a production book built on the IMGN pl
 atform: script breakdown\, schedule\, coverage\, and pre-visualization for
  the project (tutorials are provided inside the application). Eligibility:
  US-based filmmakers in the 50 states plus DC\, aged 18 and older. If the 
 project is already completed before funds are disbursed\, the grant is pai
 d as a reimbursement upon submission of a final cut of the film. Winner an
 nounced and funds disbursed by end of January 2027.\n\nOrganisation: CFA I
 nstitute (in partnership with IMGN)\nLocation: United States (50 states + 
 DC\; applicants must be 18+)\nAward: $4\,500 to one filmmaker. If the proj
 ect is already completed before funds are disbursed\, the $4\,500 is paid 
 as a reimbursement upon submission of a final cut.\nDuration: Per project\
 ; winner announced and funds disbursed by end of January 2027\n\nMore: htt
 ps://form.jotform.com/CFA_Institute/cfa--imgn-grant-application?utm_source
 =artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://form.jotform.com/CFA_Institute/cfa--imgn-grant-application?utm_
 source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:United States (50 states + DC\; applicants must be 18+)
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:cafe-royal-performance-grant-summer-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260905T151909Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260803
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260804
SUMMARY:Deadline: Cafe Royal Cultural Foundation: Performance Grant (Summer
  2026)
DESCRIPTION:The Cafe Royal Cultural Foundation NYC Performance Grant suppor
 ts individual artists producing theatre\, dance and performance art (which
  may be accompanied by music) moving toward a public performance. The Summ
 er 2026 cycle offers up to $10\,000 for eligible production costs: actors\
 , dancers and guest performers\, director/choreographer/composer and creat
 ive-team fees\, costumes\, lighting\, sound\, scenic\, props and set\, reh
 earsal and venue rental\, and production and technical staff. Funds do not
  cover the creation or composition of new work\; if the performance is a p
 lay it must be a full production (readings do not qualify). ELIGIBILITY: i
 ndividual performers only (not groups or organizations) who are US citizen
 s or resident aliens\, current NYC residents who have lived in the city at
  least one year and will remain through project completion. This cycle acc
 epts only 40 applications and closes early if that cap is reached. Applica
 nts may submit up to 5 video links. Apply via the foundation's performance
  page. Deadline: 3 August 2026\, 9:00 am ET (or earlier if 40 applications
  are received).\n\nOrganisation: Cafe Royal Cultural Foundation NYC\nLocat
 ion: New York City (five boroughs)\, or a location in close proximity to N
 YC approved by the foundation\, USA\nAward: Up to $10\,000 for production 
 costs (performers\, collaborators\, rehearsal and venue rental\, design el
 ements\, technical staff\, etc.). Does not fund the creation or compositio
 n of new work. No application fee.\nDuration: Production grant\; performan
 ce must start no sooner than 1 November 2026 and complete by 22 September 
 2027\n\nMore: https://caferoyalculturalfoundation.org/performance-page?utm
 _source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://caferoyalculturalfoundation.org/performance-page?utm_source=art
 ificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:New York City (five boroughs)\, or a location in close proximity t
 o NYC approved by the foundation\, USA
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:pandora-short-film-grant-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260905T151909Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261001
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261002
SUMMARY:Deadline: Pandora Short Film Grant 2026
DESCRIPTION:The Pandora Short Film Grant is a 501(c)(3) nonprofit that awar
 ds an annual $12\,000 grant to an early-career US filmmaker for the produc
 tion of a narrative short film. Projects at script stage or already filmed
  and seeking finishing funds are both eligible. The winner also receives a
  Panavision camera package (about $15\,000)\, an optional $1\,000 Kodak Fi
 lm Grant\, mentorship from an award-winning filmmaker (2026 mentors includ
 e Alma Har'el\, Lila Neugebauer\, Lauren Wolkstein\, Sarah Elizabeth Mintz
  and Chris Merola)\, and fiscal sponsorship\; three finalists receive ment
 orship\, fiscal sponsorship\, Panavision New Filmmaker Program considerati
 on and discounted production insurance. ELIGIBILITY: open to all early-car
 eer US filmmakers\; applicants are asked NOT to apply if they have directe
 d a feature film with a budget over $250K or directed or produced televisi
 on. Selection is blind\, based on script and pitch materials\, weighting a
 rtistic vision over industry resume. TIMELINE: submissions open 30 May 202
 6\, close 1 October 2026\, notifications 15 November 2026.\n\nOrganisation
 : Pandora Short Film Grant\nLocation: United States (early-career US filmm
 akers)\nAward: $12\,000 production grant to the winner\, plus a Panavision
  camera package (worth about $15\,000)\, an optional $1\,000 Kodak Film Gr
 ant toward film stock and lab expenses\, mentorship from an award-winning 
 filmmaker\, and fiscal sponsorship. Three finalists receive mentorship\, f
 iscal sponsorship\, consideration for the Panavision New Filmmaker Program
 \, and $500 off Athos production insurance. ENTRY FEE: tiered\, $30 regula
 r ($27 Gold members) and $40 late ($36)\; Pandora states fee waivers are a
 vailable on request.\nDuration: Production grant for a narrative short fil
 m (script stage or finishing funds)\n\nMore: https://pandora.org/?utm_sour
 ce=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://pandora.org/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=r
 eferral
LOCATION:United States (early-career US filmmakers)
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:legalization-project-immigrant-content-creators-2026@artificialnouveau.
 github.io
DTSTAMP:20260905T151909Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260817
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260818
SUMMARY:Deadline: The Legalization Project: Immigrant Content Creators Prog
 ram 2026-27
DESCRIPTION:The Legalization Project is running a nine-month programme for 
 experienced content creators who want to make engaging\, accessible work a
 bout U.S. immigration law\, policy and the issues affecting immigrant comm
 unities. Rather than commissioning one-off pieces\, it pairs a USD 10\,000
  stipend with actual legal grounding: training in U.S. immigration law and
  policy\, ongoing updates from immigration attorneys and advocates\, and c
 ontinuous feedback on the content you make. This suits creators already wo
 rking in video\, social or short-form documentary who want to report accur
 ately on a fast-moving legal area rather than guess at it. ELIGIBILITY: ex
 perienced content creators who are immigrants. The programme is explicitly
  open to immigrants of ALL statuses. APPLICATION: preliminary applications
  are due 17 August 2026\, through the programme page at legalizationprojec
 t.notion.site/icc-program (also circulated as bit.ly/icc-program). Being a
  preliminary application\, expect a second stage after this date.\n\nOrgan
 isation: The Legalization Project\nLocation: United States focus (content 
 covers U.S. immigration law and policy). Programme delivery format not spe
 cified in the call.\nAward: USD 10\,000 stipend\, plus training in U.S. im
 migration law and policy\, regular updates from immigration attorneys and 
 advocates\, and ongoing feedback and support on your content. No applicati
 on fee.\nDuration: Nine months\n\nMore: https://legalizationproject.notion
 .site/icc-program?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referra
 l
URL:https://legalizationproject.notion.site/icc-program?utm_source=artifici
 alnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:United States focus (content covers U.S. immigration law and polic
 y). Programme delivery format not specified in the call.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:laselle-travel-fellowship-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260905T151909Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260831
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260901
SUMMARY:Deadline: Dorothy Antoinette LaSelle Travel Fellowship 2026
DESCRIPTION:The Dorothy Antoinette LaSelle Foundation established this fell
 owship in recognition of the impact travel can have on an artist's career.
  Two awards of USD 5\,000 go to artists working and residing in Texas\, en
 abling each recipient to complete a research trip that furthers their arti
 stic practice. The fellowship echoes its namesake's own history: Dorothy A
 ntoinette 'Toni' LaSelle\, a pioneer of Modernism in Texas\, travelled to 
 Europe in 1927-28 after completing her MA at the University of Chicago\, e
 xploring the art of England\, Italy and France on a trip made possible by 
 a study fund awarded for work in the First Student Art Exhibition at the R
 enaissance Society in 1926. JUROR: this year's guest juror is Lana Meador\
 , Associate Curator of Modern and Contemporary Art at the San Antonio Muse
 um of Art\, who joined SAMA's curatorial department in 2015 and has organi
 sed more than a dozen exhibitions there\, including co-curating Texas Wome
 n: A New History of Abstract Art (2020) and the ongoing Gateway series of 
 commissioned site-specific murals. She has juried artist awards for the Ci
 ty of San Antonio's Department of Arts and Culture\, the International Col
 or Awards\, Texas Sculpture Group and the Utah Artist Fellowship. ELIGIBIL
 ITY: must currently reside in Texas and have lived there for the past year
 \; must be a practising professional with at least three solo or group exh
 ibitions in the past three years (BFA and juried exhibitions count)\; and 
 must currently hold a degree in the arts. APPLICATION MATERIALS: image thu
 mbnails of a maximum of four works with title\, date\, medium and dimensio
 ns (JPEG or JPG only\, at least 1\,200 pixels on the longest side and unde
 r 5MB each)\; an artist CV\; a reference contact list with two names\, con
 tact details and relationship to the artist\; a project proposal of 2\,000
  characters or less covering why the fellowship matters to your practice\,
  your goals for the travel\, and when the proposed travel would take place
 \; and a copy of a valid Texas driver's licence or other proof of one year
  of Texas residency. All information and communications are in English\, w
 ith translation assistance available on request. Applications opened 3 Aug
 ust 2026\; awardees are notified 2 November 2026. Questions: info@lasellef
 oundation.org. Deadline 31 August 2026 at 11:59pm CDT.\n\nOrganisation: Do
 rothy Antoinette LaSelle Foundation\nLocation: Texas\, USA. Applicants mus
 t currently reside in Texas and have lived there for the past year. The fu
 nded research trip itself may be anywhere.\nAward: Two awards of USD 5\,00
 0 each\, distributed in the fall of 2026\, to fund a research trip that fu
 rthers the artist's practice. No application fee.\nDuration: Travel must b
 e completed between 1 December 2026 and 31 December 2027\n\nMore: https://
 www.lasellefoundation.org/travel-fellowship?utm_source=artificialnouveaugr
 antdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.lasellefoundation.org/travel-fellowship?utm_source=artifici
 alnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Texas\, USA. Applicants must currently reside in Texas and have li
 ved there for the past year. The funded research trip itself may be anywhe
 re.
END:VEVENT
